// Tailscale ACL for tailnet `balizero` (tail461666.ts.net) — policy as code.
//
// STATUS: PROPOSED. Not yet applied. The tailnet currently runs the DEFAULT allow-all policy;
// applying this file is an operator[GUI] action (admin console -> Access Controls), because the
// fleet holds no Tailscale API token and the CLI can neither read nor write ACLs. Apply runbook:
// docs/runbooks/tailnet-acl-apply.md. Ledger: .claude/skills/modus/PENDING-ARMS.md.
//
// ============================================================================
// SHELL-ROUTE: the reason this file exists. Do not delete this block.
// ============================================================================
// Measured on the live tailnet 2026-08-11 from M5 (`tailscale debug netmap`), re-confirmed
// 2026-08-29: the packet filter is ONE rule — every node -> 0.0.0.0/0, ports 0-65535,
// TCP+UDP+ICMP. On top of that, `tailscale serve` on Pro publishes TAILNET-WIDE and with NO
// authentication:
//
//     https://nuzantara.tail461666.ts.net/        -> 127.0.0.1:18789   OpenClaw Control
//     https://nuzantara.tail461666.ts.net/term    -> 127.0.0.1:7681    ttyd -p 7681 -W zsh
//     https://nuzantara.tail461666.ts.net/deck    -> 127.0.0.1:18890   vision deck
//     https://nuzantara.tail461666.ts.net/cinema  -> 127.0.0.1:18891   vision cinema
//
// `-W` means WRITABLE and there is no `-c user:pass` credential flag, so `/term` is an
// unauthenticated writable zsh shell on Pro as user `nuzantara` — the host holding the wa-mirror
// raw PII, the CRM runtime and ~/.nuzantara-secrets.env. GET /term answered 200 from another node.
//
// ttyd binds 127.0.0.1, which protects it from the LAN and the internet but NOT from the tailnet:
// `tailscale serve` is the proxy that carries it to every peer. A localhost bind is therefore not
// a mitigation here, and any future reader tempted to write it off as "it's only on loopback"
// should stop at this paragraph.
//
// The exposure is reachable ONLY over port 443 on `pro`. So port 443 on `pro` is the shell port,
// and every rule below that grants it names its sources one host at a time — never a wildcard,
// never a whole user's device set, never a group. Two rules grant it (m5, and Zero's three iOS
// devices) and both are Zero's own hardware. `tag:team-device` appears in no rule's `src` anywhere in
// this file, which is what keeps a teammate's laptop off that shell.
//
// This is NOT a live breach today: all six nodes belong to `antonellosiano@gmail.com`. It is a
// hard blocker on the tailnet team expansion. ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING — apply this policy
// BEFORE any team device joins; join-then-restrict leaves a window in which that device holds
// the shell. Hardening ttyd itself (`-c user:pass`, or dropping `/term` from `serve`) changes how
// Zero reaches his own machine from his own phone, so it stays his call and stays in the ledger;
// this file contains the blast radius without touching his workflow.
// ============================================================================
//
// WHAT CHANGED 2026-08-29 (this revision): the previous draft granted
// `antonellosiano@gmail.com` -> `antonellosiano@gmail.com:*` — every port on every owned device.
// That reproduced the flat default among the fleet and is not deny-by-default in any meaningful
// sense: it left `pro:443` open to all six nodes, and it would silently absorb any new service on
// any new port. It is replaced by the per-direction, per-port matrix below, derived by auditing
// what the repo actually connects to (evidence cited on each rule). Nothing in that matrix is a
// guess: a rule with no cited consumer is not in this file.
//
// WHAT THIS COSTS THE EXISTING FLEET: the flows below are the ones the repo has consumers for.
// A flow that exists on the machines but nowhere in the repo will break, loudly, on first use —
// that is the intended failure mode of deny-by-default, and the runbook's verification step is
// where it should surface. Add the missing rule with its evidence; do not widen a rule to `:*`.
//
// NOT GOVERNED BY THIS FILE, stated so nobody looks for it here:
//   * Internet egress. Each node runs its own `fly proxy 15432:5432` to Fly.io over the public
//     internet; ACLs govern tailnet traffic, not egress.
//   * The public Funnel. `nuzantara.tail461666.ts.net:8443` -> 127.0.0.1:8789 (the WhatsApp
//     webhook, public by design because Meta must reach it) is a Funnel, and Funnel traffic is
//     not filtered by these ACLs. Port 443 — the one carrying /term — is NOT funnelled.
//   * `scripts/fleet_watch.py`'s liveness probes. CORRECTED 2026-08-29 (round-2 gate): an
//     earlier revision said fleet_watch has a "ping probe" that survives because disco pings are
//     not ACL-gated. It has no ping probe at all — `check_tailscale()` reads the LOCAL daemon
//     (`tailscale status --json`, no tailnet traffic) and `check_ssh()` uses OpenSSH on port 22,
//     which rules 1 and 2 grant. The conclusion held; the cited mechanism did not exist.
//   * LAN/mDNS paths (`pro-lan`, `Nuzantara.local`). Not tailnet traffic.
//
// ONE DELIBERATE NARROWING, declared rather than slipped in: the compiled SSH policy in effect
// today is `action: accept` with `sshUsers {"*": "=", "root": "root"}` and agent + local + remote
// port forwarding, principals = every node IP. That is passwordless ROOT over Tailscale SSH from
// any tailnet device with no re-authentication. It is LATENT — no node runs the Tailscale SSH
// server (`RunSSH: false` on M5; Pro advertises no SSH host key; Mini's inbound path is unusable,
// see README). Because it is latent, dropping the root grant costs zero disruption today and
// disarms it before someone runs `tailscale up --ssh` and arms it by accident. Fleet SSH in daily
// use is OpenSSH on port 22 with keys, which the rules below grant explicitly.
//
// VALIDATION LIMIT, stated rather than implied. This file is checked repo-side by
// scripts/tests/test_tailnet_acl_deny_by_default.py, and that check covers LESS than a reader
// would assume, so here is its exact reach (every line below re-measured against audit_policy()
// on 2026-08-29 after a cross-family review found an earlier version of THIS PARAGRAPH overstating
// in six places — the irony is recorded deliberately).
//
//   VERIFIED, on the `acls` and `grants` blocks (the team-reaching source check below is the one
//   item that also applies to `ssh`): no `*` as a source; no source starting
//   `tag:team` and no `autogroup:tagged`; no `*` as a destination host or port; no destination
//   spanning more than 64 ports; every destination selector resolves to a node in `hosts` or is
//   refused (UNRESOLVABLE_DST_SELECTOR) — with exactly ONE permitted exception, `tag:team-device`,
//   which has no IP until a laptop enrols; pro:443 reachable only from the FOUR allowlisted nodes
//   (m5, iphone-14, iphone175, apple-vision-pro), in every spelling of node and port. On `acls`
//   rules only, `proto` must be present. File-wide: no unknown top-level key; the tokens
//   `SHELL-ROUTE:`, 7681, /term and ttyd present SOMEWHERE in the file; and a deny-test covering
//   the shell port for a team-reaching source.
//
//   BARELY CHECKED — THE `ssh` BLOCK BELOW gets exactly three checks: the literal string "root" in
//   `users`, a team-reaching `src`, and a team-reaching `dst`. None of the wildcard, port or
//   selector-resolution checks above are applied to it. Measured: an ssh rule reading
//   `"src": ["*"], "dst": ["*"], "users": ["autogroup:nonroot"]` produces zero findings, where the
//   same wildcard in an `acls` rule produces WILDCARD_SRC + WILDCARD_DST_HOST +
//   UNRESOLVABLE_DST_SELECTOR. And the root check is a denylist of that one literal, so
//   `"users": ["*"]` — strictly wider than root — is green. THEREFORE: a green guard does NOT
//   establish that this file grants no root over Tailscale SSH. The narrowing declared in the
//   paragraph above is real, but only READING the ssh block proves it — it must say
//   `"users": ["autogroup:nonroot"]`.
//
//   ALSO NOT VERIFIED. (1) There is no UNRESOLVABLE_SRC_SELECTOR mirroring the destination one:
//   the source column is checked for the literal `*` and for team-reaching selectors only, so
//   `tag:fleet` or `group:ops` as a src measures clean against an ordinary destination (`mini:22`)
//   while the same `tag:fleet` as a DST yields UNRESOLVABLE_DST_SELECTOR. One carve-out, so the gap
//   is not overstated: a src aimed at the SHELL PORT is separately required to be a named node
//   (SHELL_PORT_SRC_NOT_A_NAMED_HOST fires for `tag:fleet -> pro:443`), so the hole is on every
//   OTHER destination. What is caught is the PREFIX `tag:team`, not the exact string, so
//   `tag:team-other` is caught and `tag:fleet` is not. (2) The shell anchor is whatever
//   `hosts["pro"]` says; no assertion pins it to 100.107.22.111 (that address appears in the guard
//   only in comments and one canonicalisation test's fixture). With "pro" moved to Mini's IP and a
//   second alias carrying Pro's real one, `mini -> <that alias>:443` measures zero findings, where
//   `mini -> pro:443` on a correct map gives SHELL_PORT_SRC_NOT_ALLOWLISTED — so the `hosts` map is
//   load-bearing and unguarded. (3) The SHELL-ROUTE token check scans the WHOLE FILE, not this
//   block: deleting the block's body passes as long as 7681 / /term / ttyd survive anywhere, and
//   this very paragraph now contains them. (4) MISSING_PROTO tests that `proto` is PRESENT, never
//   its value.
//
// All of these carry their reproduction in .claude/skills/modus/PENDING-ARMS.md (search
// `tailnet-acl guard`). They are a specified follow-up PR, not a blocker on applying this policy.
//
// And none of this is ACCEPTANCE. The file parses as JSON with comments stripped; that is grammar
// and intent. There is no Tailscale API token on the fleet and no offline validator, so the ONLY
// authority on whether this policy is accepted is the admin console at save time, which also runs
// the `tests` block. If it refuses, read its message before editing anything here.
//
// CORRECTED 2026-08-29 (round-1 grader, finding 9): earlier revisions of this file warned that
// `autogroup:member` is invalid as a `dst`. That is false for an ordinary ACL destination, where
// current Tailscale syntax permits it. It IS invalid as an **ssh** `dst` — which is the only
// place this file would have used it, and why the ssh block below uses `autogroup:self`. Keep
// the distinction; do not restore the blanket claim.
{
  // Magic IPs are stable per node and are not secrets. Used as both rule operands and test
  // targets. If a node is removed and re-registered it gets a NEW magic IP and its rules stop
  // matching — the service breaks loudly rather than silently widening, and the fix is to update
  // this block. Source: `tailscale status --json`, README table, re-read 2026-08-29.
  "hosts": {
    "pro":              "100.107.22.111",
    "mini":             "100.93.236.6",
    "m5":               "100.110.186.116",
    "iphone-14":        "100.113.83.92",
    "iphone175":        "100.77.16.7",
    "apple-vision-pro": "100.97.28.18"
  },

  // A tagged device is owned by the TAILNET, not by a user, so it is deliberately excluded from
  // `autogroup:member` — that exclusion is half the mechanism. The other half is that `tag:team-device`
  // appears in no rule's `src`, anywhere in this file.
  "tagOwners": {
    // A teammate's company laptop. Reusable — the mechanism is person-neutral on purpose, and
    // this repo is public, so no device is mapped to a named person here.
    "tag:team-device": ["autogroup:admin"],
    // Reserved for a future fleet node that should be tailnet-owned rather than user-owned (a
    // headless server, a CI runner). Nothing carries it today; it is declared so that adding such
    // a node is a tag assignment plus one rule, not a re-think of this file.
    "tag:fleet": ["autogroup:admin"]
  },

  "acls": [
    // ---- 1. Pro -> Mini. Pro is the hub; Mini is the workhorse. ------------------------------
    //   22    OpenSSH: memory-sync hub->spoke (infra/sync-daemons/memory-sync-bidirectional.sh),
    //         fleet-watch liveness probe (scripts/fleet_watch.py), the intake-qwen autocatalog
    //         tunnel (scripts/intake_qwen_autocatalog_worker.sh forwards 11434 over ssh), and
    //         infra/launchagents/install_fly_pg_tunnel.sh reading Pro's own keychain.
    //   6379  Redis EventBus, password-gated since 2026-06-29 (infra/eventbus/publisher.py).
    //   11434 Ollama on Mini — WR2 image VLM scorer
    //         (infra/launchagents/com.balizero.wr2.image-generator.plist) and the MOS+
    //         compression worker (scripts/mos-plus-compression-worker.py).
    //   8990  Mata Garuda KG query API (infra/launchagents/com.matagaruda.kg-query-api.plist,
    //         consumed by apps/nuzantara-mcp/nuzantara_mcp/tools/kg_intel.py).
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "proto":  "tcp",
      "src":    ["pro"],
      "dst":    ["mini:22", "mini:6379", "mini:11434", "mini:8990"]
    },

    // ---- 2. Mini -> Pro. Only SSH. -----------------------------------------------------------
    //   22    fleet-watch's reverse liveness probe (infra/fleet-watch/peers.json declares the
    //         probe mutual) and wr2-warroom-sync pulling war-room output from Pro
    //         (infra/launchagents/wrappers/wr2-warroom-sync.sh).
    // Deliberately NOT granted: pro:443. Mini has no consumer for the deck, the cinema or the
    // shell, and it is the least-attended machine on the fleet. See SHELL-ROUTE above.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "proto":  "tcp",
      "src":    ["mini"],
      "dst":    ["pro:22"]
    },

    // ---- 3. Pro -> M5. Only SSH. -------------------------------------------------------------
    //   22    memory-sync hub->spoke; M5 is a spoke (memory-sync-bidirectional.sh names
    //         air-m5-2.tail461666.ts.net). Spokes never talk to each other.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "proto":  "tcp",
      "src":    ["pro"],
      "dst":    ["m5:22"]
    },

    // ---- 4. M5 -> Pro. SSH plus the serve endpoints. -----------------------------------------
    //   22    wr2-queue-pull, M5's scheduled pull of the WR2 queue
    //         (infra/launchagents/wrappers/wr2-queue-pull.sh), plus interactive `ssh pro`, which
    //         is how the primary workstation reaches the runtime host at all.
    //   443   `tailscale serve`: OpenClaw Control, /deck, /cinema — AND /term.
    //         THIS GRANT INCLUDES THE UNAUTHENTICATED WRITABLE SHELL. It is granted because M5 is
    //         Zero's own workstation and the deck is used from it. It is granted to M5 by name,
    //         not to a user, a group or a wildcard, precisely so that widening it later takes a
    //         deliberate edit to this line. See SHELL-ROUTE above.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "proto":  "tcp",
      "src":    ["m5"],
      "dst":    ["pro:22", "pro:443"]
    },

    // ---- 5. M5 -> Mini. ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    //   22    interactive `ssh mini` from the primary workstation (the alias is declared in
    //         CLAUDE.md's machine table and in infra/fleet/nodes.json).
    //   4317  Zero Design Studio local-webapp-server, the wr2-control-app client's endpoint
    //         (apps/wr2-control-app/Sources/Theme.swift).
    // Deliberately NOT granted: mini:11434 and mini:6379. Every Ollama and Redis consumer found
    // in the repo runs on Pro. If an M5-side consumer is added, add the port here with its
    // evidence rather than widening this rule.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "proto":  "tcp",
      "src":    ["m5"],
      "dst":    ["mini:22", "mini:4317"]
    },

    // ---- 6. Zero's iOS devices -> Pro, port 443 only. -----------------------------------------
    // The Apple Vision Pro and the two iPhones consume /deck and /cinema; that is the entire
    // point of the vision-deck pilot
    // (research/operations/2026-08-11-apple-vision-pro-tailnet-leverage.md). They get 443 and
    // nothing else — no SSH, no Redis, no Ollama.
    // THIS GRANT ALSO INCLUDES /term, for the same reason as rule 4: these are Zero's own devices
    // and port 443 cannot be split by URL path in a Tailscale ACL. Path-level separation would
    // mean moving /term onto its own port in Pro's `tailscale serve` config — a change to Pro,
    // not to this file. That is the real fix and it is filed in the ledger.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "proto":  "tcp",
      "src":    ["iphone-14", "iphone175", "apple-vision-pro"],
      "dst":    ["pro:443"]
    },

    // ---- 7. A team device is a DESTINATION for support, never a source. -----------------------
    // This is the direction the 2026-05-02 decision actually asked for ("Bali Zero retains remote
    // access se serve"): we reach the laptop, the laptop reaches nothing. 22 is SSH, 5900 is
    // Screen Sharing, per infra/tailscale/enroll-team-device.md.
    //
    // DECLARED CASUALTY (round-2 gate): `scripts/profile-monitor/` is a repo-resident consumer in
    // exactly the direction this rule fences. An employee's Mac POSTs checkout events to
    // `http://100.107.22.111:9099/checkout` (mac-client/profile-monitor.swift:12; the wrapper
    // listens on 9099, wrapper.py:44) and mac-client/setup-balizero.sh is the documented procedure
    // for joining such a Mac to this tailnet. Under this policy a `tag:team-device` Mac CANNOT
    // reach 9099, and the failure is INVISIBLE TO US — scoped precisely (round-2 cross-family review,
    // 2026-08-29; an earlier draft called the POST "fire-and-forget", which the source contradicts):
    // the client sets a 5s request timeout and blocks on a semaphore up to 6s, and a DROPPED POST —
    // this policy's case — times out as a transport error and IS recorded as `POST_FAIL`, into
    // `~/Library/Logs/balizero-profile-events.log` ON THE EMPLOYEE'S OWN MAC
    // (`mac-client/profile-monitor.swift:48-79`). Only transport errors are classified that way: an
    // HTTP 4xx/5xx logs `POST_OK` carrying the status, and a semaphore expiry logs nothing at all. Nothing on the fleet reads that file, so from this
    // side a dropped POST is indistinguishable from "no checkout happened", on an HR-monitoring
    // surface. The evidence exists; it is on the far side of the fence this policy raises. This is not
    // an oversight, but state its cost ACCURATELY rather than inflating it (corrected 2026-08-29 —
    // an earlier draft of this comment called the absence "the one thing that keeps the shell
    // fenced", which is false as Tailscale semantics and biased the owner's decision). ACL rules
    // are independent allow entries: a rule granting `tag:team-device -> pro:9099` with this file's
    // mandatory `"proto": "tcp"` grants TCP 9099 and nothing else — it does not open pro:443. (The
    // `proto` qualifier is load-bearing, not decoration: omit it and the same rule would also carry
    // UDP/9099 and ICMP for that node pair.) What actually fences the shell is that NO rule
    // grants a team device pro:443, and a named single-port exception would not change that.
    // The real cost is the tripwire, not the shell: `tag:team-device` in any `src` fires
    // TEAM_TAG_AS_SOURCE, so the guard goes red and the file loses a coarse structural invariant
    // that needs no per-port reasoning to check. That is a genuine loss and a defensible reason to
    // refuse — it is simply a smaller one than "the shell opens". Whether to grant it as a single
    // named exception (with its own deny-tests for every other port, pro:443 included) or to move
    // that client off the tailnet is a business decision (Legge 5), so it is declared here and left
    // to the owner rather than resolved by this diff.
    //
    // There is deliberately NO rule anywhere in this file with `tag:team-device` in `src`. That single
    // absence is what denies a teammate's laptop the shell on pro:443, the Redis on mini:6379,
    // the Ollama on mini:11434, every sshd, and the CRM runtime — at the packet layer, before the
    // CRM's own RBAC (which is row-level, and would otherwise be bypassed underneath) gets a say.
    // scripts/tests/test_tailnet_acl_deny_by_default.py goes RED if that absence ends. Stated
    // precisely (round-1 grader, finding 5): `check-tailnet-acl` is NOT a required check yet, so
    // a red there is a loud signal on the PR, not a merge block. Promoting it to required is a
    // branch-protection change and therefore operator-only.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "proto":  "tcp",
      "src":    ["m5", "pro"],
      "dst":    ["tag:team-device:22", "tag:team-device:5900"]
    }
  ],

  // Tailscale SSH: preserved for the fleet, root removed (see the note above), never granted
  // toward or from `tag:team-device`. `dst: autogroup:self` is the shape Tailscale's own default policy
  // uses with an `autogroup:member` src; it resolves to exactly the fleet here, since every node
  // is owned by the same account and `tag:team-device` devices are tailnet-owned, not user-owned, so
  // they fall outside `autogroup:member` on both sides.
  "ssh": [
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src":    ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst":    ["autogroup:self"],
      "users":  ["autogroup:nonroot"]
    }
  ],

  // The console validates these on save. Both directions are pinned: a policy shipped with only
  // "accept" tests is the same half-guard mistake the 2026-05 handoff runbook made — it could
  // report success and never failure.
  "tests": [
    // Innocence: the flows the fleet actually runs must survive the narrowing.
    {
      "src":    "pro",
      "accept": ["mini:22", "mini:6379", "mini:11434", "mini:8990", "m5:22"]
    },
    {
      "src":    "m5",
      "accept": ["pro:22", "pro:443", "mini:22", "mini:4317"]
    },
    {
      "src":    "mini",
      "accept": ["pro:22"]
    },
    {
      "src":    "apple-vision-pro",
      "accept": ["pro:443"]
    },

    // Guilt: the narrowing must actually deny. A team laptop must not open anything on the
    // fleet — least of all pro:443, which is where the unauthenticated writable shell lives.
    {
      "src":  "tag:team-device",
      "deny": ["pro:443", "pro:22", "pro:18789", "mini:22", "mini:6379", "mini:11434",
               "mini:8990", "m5:22"]
    },
    {
      // Mini is Zero's own machine and still does not get the shell: least privilege is not a
      // property of who owns the device, it is a property of what the device has a consumer for.
      "src":  "mini",
      "deny": ["pro:443", "pro:18789", "m5:22"]
    },
    {
      // An iOS device gets the deck and nothing else.
      "src":  "iphone-14",
      "deny": ["pro:22", "mini:22", "mini:11434", "m5:22"]
    },
    {
      // The SUPPORT direction, asserted rather than assumed. A test destination may be a tag,
      // so this needs no enrolled laptop — see the note at the foot of this file.
      "src":    "m5",
      "accept": ["tag:team-device:22", "tag:team-device:5900"]
    },
    {
      // The blanket the previous draft granted is gone: no port on Pro beyond 22 and 443 is
      // reachable from M5, so a new service appearing on a new port is denied until someone adds
      // it here on purpose.
      "src":  "m5",
      "deny": ["pro:6379", "pro:18789", "pro:18890", "pro:7681", "mini:6379", "mini:11434"]
    }
  ]

  // CORRECTED 2026-08-29 (round-1 grader, finding 4): an earlier revision of this file claimed
  // the accept direction toward a team laptop could not be tested until the node existed,
  // "because ACL tests need a concrete destination host". That is false — a test destination may
  // be a TAG — so the support path is asserted above (`m5` and `pro` accept `tag:team-device:22`
  // and `:5900`) with no enrolled laptop required. It was the one claim in this file that
  // excused an untested direction, which is exactly the shape of excuse worth distrusting.
  // After enrollment, add the concrete-host form too — that is step 5 of
  // infra/tailscale/enroll-team-device.md:
  //   {"src": "m5", "accept": ["team-laptop-01:22", "team-laptop-01:5900"]}
  // with `"team-laptop-01": "<its magic IP>"` in `hosts`.
}
